approaches:
The proposed aims are:
to train longitudinal multivariate models that identify latent biological aging axes across the menopause transition;
to determine whether these axes are better aligned with chronological age, menopausal stage, or time relative to the final menstrual period;
to interpret the biological composition of the axes by identifying the strongest contributing biomarkers and clinical variables;
to test whether the axes are associated with later-life outcomes, including cardiometabolic risk, bone health, physical-function decline, frailty-related measures, and mortality where available; and
to prepare the model for future validation in independent cohorts, including and local Singaporean clinical cohorts.

Datasets: All of Us Registered Tier and Controlled Tier whole‑genome sequencing. 
Methods: (1) variant annotation and carrier identification for cardiomyopathy associated genes; 
(2) cohort construction and phenotype validation for cardiomyopathy, heart failure, arrhythmias, and conduction disease; 
(3) age‑specific penetrance and Cox/time‑to‑event modeling; 
(4) modifier analyses—polygenic risk scores and common‑variant screens within carriers—and gene‑environment tests using wearable, medication, and lifestyle exposures; 
(5) cross‑ancestry calibration, missing‑data handling, and sensitivity checks. Tools: AoU Researcher Workbench (Python/R notebooks), BigQuery for cohort queries, and standard genetics libraries available in the enclave (e.g., PLINK/Hail). 
All work remains inside the enclave; only AoU‑approved aggregate outputs are exported.

